hot-3 — Environment frame representation (plan-3).

Make the environment a flat, index-addressable structure so hot-2's addresses are
cheap to follow.

WHAT
- Represent a lexical frame as a small VECTOR of slots (not an alist of
  name->value/local-variable structs): frame = { parent, slots[n] }, indexed by the
  hot-2 slot index.  Allocate the slot vector sized by the analyzed local count.
- Keep the SPECIAL/dynamic binding stack SEPARATE (a shadowing stack of
  symbol/old-value pairs restored on unwind), so lexical access never walks dynamic
  bindings and special access never walks lexical frames.  DEFVAR/PROGV/LET-of-a-
  special push there.
- Preserve the debugger's per-frame info (the call frame records function +
  environment for backtraces — see [[bocl-defun-lexical-block]]): keep enough
  metadata on the frame for backtrace even with vector slots.
- GC: frames are ordinary heap objects (non-moving GC keeps raw slot pointers valid);
  auto-rooted via allocate().  Watch the write barrier when storing into an older
  frame (cf. gc_write_barrier usage).

ACCEPTANCE: binding/closure/special-variable suites (control, restarts, must-control)
green; measured allocation/lookup reduction vs the alist frames; no GC regressions
(run BOCL_GCMINORSTRESS-style checks where applicable, mindful of the known
minor-gc-doomed issue).

DEPENDS ON: hot-2.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.7.2, hot branch) — allocation-reduction acceptance met; vector-frame
form deferred with the analyzer.

Delivered: lexical bindings are now plain conses (name . value) instead of two-
allocation CLOS structure instances (see the v1.7.2 commit).  ACCEPTANCE metrics:
binding/closure/special suites green (42/42); measured allocation reduction — a
call-heavy micro-benchmark went 679,180 -> 599,165 allocations (-12%), GC
13.1% -> 11.9% (no regression).

NOT done: the "frame = a VECTOR of slots indexed by the hot-2 (depth,index)
address" form, because it requires the hot-2 analyzer (deferred to co-develop
with vm-3).  The cons representation captures the allocation/lookup-cost win
without the analyzer.

KEY FINDING (measured, applies to hot-4 too): the allocation reduction is
TIME-NEUTRAL.  Even in allocation-churny loop code, eval (CPU) is ~88% and GC
~12%, and lexical-binding wrappers are only a fraction of allocations, so cutting
them saves ~1-2% wall-clock.  bocl's tree-walker is CPU-per-operation bound, not
allocation bound (the v1.7.0 accessor win — pure CPU — gave 2.6x; this alloc win
gave ~0%).  Implication: the arg-STACK of hot-4 will similarly yield ~0% from the
avoided consing itself; its only real TIME win is the CPU it removes (the callee
list-walk + the per-call lambda-list re-parse), which is a smaller, contained
gain.  The large eval lever remains plan-2 (byte-code VM), exactly as hot-1
concluded.
